  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "New York State Assembly committees held a public hearing at the Capitol to examine standards for Automated License Plate Readers, focusing on data privacy, information sharing, and consumer protections. Law enforcement defended ALPRs as crime-solving tools, while protesters outside raised concerns about private vendor data ownership and misuse, including an alleged incident of an Albany County Sheriff's deputy using cameras to spy on an ex-lover.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The way the contract works for Columbia County is the private company is actually allowed to do whatever it wants, it owns that data that New York state is paying for, and it can do whatever it wants, and that's a huge risk.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "Right now, there are no universal minimum standards for data usage and sharing by the license plate reader systems in New York.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
